= Fennel

_Greco-Roman / European: fully classical — fennel (maráthron / foeniculum) is one of the major aromatic Apiaceae drugs of Dioscorides, Pliny, and the medieval European pharmacy, with a particularly rich Hildegardian and monastic-medicine register. Ayurveda: classical under the saṃhitā name_ śatapuṣpā / madhurikā _(with substantial commentarial-identification debate between fennel and dill; the modern Indian_ saunf _is fennel). Chinese: classical — 小茴香 (xiǎohuíxiāng) is a Bencao drug,_ distinct from _the canonical 八角茴香 / 大茴香 star anise (Illicium verum), and also from European anise (Pimpinella anisum) of the_ anise _entry. This is the canonical Mediterranean–Chinese–Indian carminative aromatic, with cleaner cross-system convergence than almost any other umbellifer._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Foeniculum vulgare_ Mill., family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae); the drug is the dried *ripe fruit* (a schizocarp, classically called "fennel seed"; botanically a fruit), with the *essential oil* (Foeniculi aetheroleum) as the principal pharmaceutical form. Two subspecies / varieties of practical importance: *_F. vulgare_ subsp. _vulgare_ var. _vulgare_* (bitter fennel, the wild type, higher in fenchone) and *_F. vulgare_ subsp. _vulgare_ var. _dulce_* (sweet fennel, the cultivated form, higher in anethole and dominant in commerce). *_Florence fennel_* (_F. vulgare_ var. _azoricum_) is the bulb-vegetable cultivar. _Foeniculum_ is the diminutive of Latin _foenum_ ("hay"), referring to the feathery hay-like leaves. _vulgare_ = "common, ordinary."#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _foeniculum_, _foenum_). The _foenum_ ("hay") → _foeniculum_ ("little hay") naming is the standard derivation, for the appearance of the feathery leaf. Species and cultivar identity: _Flora Europaea_; the bitter / sweet / Florence cultivar distinctions are standard.]

*English:* _Fennel_, from Old English _finugl_, _finol_, from Vulgar Latin _fenuc(u)lum_, from Latin _foeniculum_. The English word preserves the diminutive form transparently.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "fennel, n.").]

*Chinese:* The classical Chinese carminative-aromatic distinction is canonical and *crucial* — the same character 茴 (huí, "fennel-aromatic") modifies two completely different plants:
- *小茴香 (xiǎohuíxiāng)*, "lesser-fennel-aromatic" — *Foeniculum vulgare* (true fennel, Apiaceae).
- *大茴香 / 八角茴香 (dàhuíxiāng / bājiǎo huíxiāng)*, "greater-fennel-aromatic / eight-points-fennel-aromatic" — *Illicium verum* (star anise, Schisandraceae) — *a different plant in a different family*.
- *茴香苗 (huíxiāngmiáo)* — the green leaves of fennel.
Both 小茴香 and 八角 are canonical Chinese drugs, with overlapping but distinct profiles; they are *not* substitutes for each other in classical formulas. The pharmacy student's mistake of confusing the two is the canonical materia-medica error.#footnote[小茴香 / 大茴香 distinction: _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004) — separate entries; the conflation in popular literature is a known error pattern.]

*Sanskrit — the śatapuṣpā / madhurikā question:*
- *शतपुष्पा (śatapuṣpā)*, "she of the hundred flowers" — classical name; identified by classical commentators *variously* as dill (_Anethum graveolens_) or fennel (_Foeniculum vulgare_). The contemporary mainstream Ayurvedic / dravyaguna identification is *dill* (Anethum graveolens), with *madhurikā* as fennel.
- *मधुरिका (madhurikā)*, "the sweet one" — the principal modern Ayurvedic name for fennel (Foeniculum vulgare), agreeing with modern Hindi _saunf_.
- *मिश्रेया (miśreyā)*, *अधःपुष्पी* (_adhaḥpuṣpī_), *शालिनी* (_śālinī_) — synonyms.
- *शीतलमिश्रेया* — synonym sometimes used.
The śatapuṣpā / madhurikā identification debate is a standing issue in dravyaguna commentary; the principal modern view (P. V. Sharma) treats śatapuṣpā as dill (_Anethum graveolens_) and madhurikā as fennel.#footnote[The śatapuṣpā / madhurikā identification debate: extensively discussed in P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II (which separates śatapuṣpā = dill and madhurikā = fennel); cf. Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_. The classical commentators are not unanimous; the modern dravyaguna consensus is the cited identification.]

*Brazilian Portuguese:* the *erva-doce* ambiguity (see anise entry) applies; in southern Brazil, Portugal, and the modern São Paulo / Rio register, *erva-doce* normally refers to *fennel*; the Florence-fennel bulb is *erva-doce* / *funcho* (the latter being a clearer disambiguation). *Funcho* is the unambiguous Brazilian Portuguese for fennel.#footnote[The funcho / erva-doce / anis disambiguation in Brazilian Portuguese: Houaiss; standard culinary terminology. Funcho is the clear word for fennel.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

小茴香 is a fully canonical drug, treated in the _Bencao Gangmu_ and standard formularies; the Chinese reading is primarily *warming the Kidney channel and dispersing cold from the lower jiao*.#footnote[小茴香 entry: _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Pungent (辛).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Kidney (腎), Liver (肝), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). The *Kidney* and *Liver* channel placement (specifically the Liver lower-jiao region) — for lower abdominal / scrotal / genital cold-pain — is the distinctive Chinese reading.

*Actions & indications:*
- 散寒止痛 — disperses cold and stops pain (the cardinal use, especially for *lower-jiao cold pain* — scrotal pain, hernia pain, dysmenorrhea from cold).
- 理氣和胃 — regulates qi and harmonizes the Stomach (digestive use, parallel with the European carminative tradition).
- 暖肝祛寒 — warms the Liver and dispels cold (the classical use for cold-pattern hernia, _shàn_ 疝氣).
- *Topically:* warm fennel-seed compresses for abdominal cold-pain.

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity heat patterns, blazing fire, hypertension with heat; pregnancy at high therapeutic doses (the strong warming-moving action).#footnote[Actions and cautions: _Bencao Gangmu_ 小茴香 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Madhurikā / saunf is a classical drug, treated in the nighaṇṭu literature and central to the Indian post-meal _mukhavāsa_ (mouth-freshener) tradition.#footnote[Madhurikā in Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; cf. Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu, Rāja Nighaṇṭu. Translations: Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_ (Chaukhamba); cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _madhurikā_ entry.]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet — distinctive), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — mild), Kaṭu (कटु, pungent — mild).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous).

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — *the standout feature*. Most carminative-aromatic Apiaceae are warming; fennel is classically *cooling* in the Indian system, which makes it suitable for *Pitta-pacifying* digestive use and distinguishes it from anise, dill, and cumin. (Note: this diverges from the Chinese reading of 小茴香 as warming — a real cross-system divergence on the same plant, perhaps reflecting different cultivar use or different climatic-context dravyaguna placement.)

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of madhurikā: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_. The śīta vīrya is the defining Ayurvedic distinction — fennel is the *Pitta-pacifying* carminative aromatic, unlike anise / dill / cumin which are warming. The cross-system divergence with the Chinese (warming) reading is real and is one of the cleaner classical disagreements in materia-medica comparison.]

*Prabhāva:* No classical assignment; reasoned cross-system, the *galactagogue / lactation-promoting* and the *cool digestive antispasmodic* combination is sometimes read at prabhāva level. Flagged interpretive.

*Doṣa effect:* Tridoṣa-pacifying — pacifies Vāta (the snigdha-laghu profile and antispasmodic), Pitta (the cooling, sweet vipāka), and Kapha (mild, by the kaṭu-tikta notes). The tridoṣa profile is uncommon and underlies fennel's near-universal applicability in Indian dietetics.#footnote[Doṣa attribution: Bhāvaprakāśa; the tridoṣa-pacifying status is the modern dravyaguna consensus.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles digestion, supports _āma_-digestion.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta gas, bloating, intestinal discomfort.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic (mild).
- _Caksuṣya_ — eye-benefiting (the classical Indian use of fennel-seed water for eye washes and as a long-term eye tonic, taken with milk; very characteristic Ayurvedic register, also reflected in the European "fennel for eyesight" folk tradition).
- _Stanyajanana_ — galactagogue.
- _Mukha-vāsa_ — mouth freshener; the canonical post-meal Indian practice — sweet fennel seeds (often with rock sugar / _miśrī_) as digestive and breath-freshener.
- _Mūtrala_ — mild diuretic.
- _Garbhāśaya-uttejaka_ — uterine-stimulant (mild emmenagogue at higher doses).
- _Pittahara, dāhahara_ — relieves Pitta and burning sensations — the distinctive Ayurvedic feature.
- _Used in:_ _agnimāndya_ (digestive weakness), _ādhmāna_ (gas), _amlapitta_ (hyperacidity — distinctive Pitta-friendly digestive aromatic), _atisāra_ (mild diarrhea), _kāsa_ (mild cough), reduced lactation, breath odor, eye disorders, _ārtava-kṣaya_ (scanty menstruation).#footnote[Karma compiled from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; the eye-benefiting / _cakṣuṣya_ use is a distinctive classical Indian use, paralleled in European folk tradition; the mukhavāsa role is universal in modern Indian dietetics.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(N) Conditional cautions:* generally well-tolerated; rare reports of cooling-aggravation in deep Kapha-vāta states.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Estrogen-sensitive conditions:* trans-anethole and its dimer dianethole have weak phytoestrogenic activity; classical lactation / emmenagogue use is consistent. *Caution* in known estrogen-sensitive cancers (breast, ovarian, uterine), endometriosis, fibroids.
  - *Pregnancy:* dietary use is generally considered safe; high-dose essential-oil internal use is *not* recommended in pregnancy.
  - *Anethole / estragole genotoxicity:* sweet fennel oil has relatively low estragole; bitter fennel oil is higher; both are limited under EU food regulation. In whole-seed and standard infusion use, estragole exposure is negligible. (Sweet-fennel cultivars are the dietary register; bitter-fennel oil should be used for short courses with the estragole consideration in mind.)
  - *Apiaceae allergy*; rare anaphylaxis case reports.
  - *Anticoagulant interaction:* very mild; clinically usually insignificant.#footnote[Phytoestrogenic activity of trans-anethole: standard phytopharmacology; the cautions parallel the anise entry's. Estragole / EU regulation: EFSA opinion on estragole; sweet vs. bitter fennel cultivar distinction is the main practical lever; verify against current EFSA guidance.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Fennel is canonical in Tibb — the principal_ mufattiḥ _(deobstruent) of the Arabic tradition, central to liver-and-spleen-and-kidney-clearing formulas. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* رازيانج (_rāziyānaj_) — the canonical Tibb name, from Persian _rāziyāna_; also شمر (_shamar_) in Maghrebi usage. Persian: رازیانه (_rāziyāne_). Urdu/Hakeemi: سونف (_saunf_, from Sanskrit _śatapuṣpā / madhurikā_); رازیانہ (_rāziyāna_) in formal Unani.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _rāziyānaj_). The Tibb classification *diverges from the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya* (cooling) of madhurikā — one of the standing cross-system divergences. The Chinese 小茴香 (warming) classification converges with the Tibb reading. The Tibb-Chinese / Ayurvedic disagreement on fennel is one of the cleaner classical disagreements.

*Af'āl:* *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* (deobstruent — the principal action; for hepatic, splenic, renal blockages); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *mudirr li-l-laban* (galactagogue — the cross-cultural convergent use); *muqawwī al-baṣar* (eye-strengthening — the famous classical use, paralleling the Ayurvedic _cakṣuṣya_ and the Dioscoridean eye-tonic register); *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* (carminative); *muḥallil* (resolvent); *muqawwī al-rūḥ* (mood-elevating, modest); *mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* (emmenagogue, modest).

*Istemālāt:* hepatic and splenic obstruction; renal-and-bladder stones (cross-system convergence with Ayurvedic and modern); eye disorders; lactation insufficiency; flatulence; cold-pattern dyspepsia; amenorrhea; cough.

*Mawāne':* bilious / hot-pattern conditions; pregnancy at high-essential-oil dose; epilepsy in some sources.

*Muṣliḥ:* rose-water; sugar; vinegar.

*Badal:* anise (*anīsūn*); dill (*shibitt*).

*Tibb formulas:* *Sharbat-i Bazūrī* (the "seed-syrup," the canonical Unani diuretic-and-deobstruent compound combining fennel + caraway + cucumber-seed + cantaloupe-seed + others); *Joshānda-yi Rāziyānaj* (fennel decoction); *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd* and other Mufarriḥ compounds use fennel as supporting aromatic; *Maʿjūn-i Falāsifah* and many digestive electuaries include fennel; *Sufūf-i Bāʿūnah* — postpartum / lactation-supporting powder with fennel.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _rāziyānaj_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _saunf / rāziyāna_ entry.#footnote[Tibb rāziyānaj: Avicennan classical reading; the Hot-Dry classification divergence from Ayurvedic śīta vīrya is a standing disagreement.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *小茴香 single-herb decoction* — 3–8 g; for lower-jiao cold pain.
- *天臺烏藥散 (Tiāntái Wū Yào Sǎn, "Tiantai Lindera Powder")* — for cold-pattern hernia pain (_shàn_); 小茴香 + 烏藥 + 木香 + 高良薑 + 川楝子 + 巴豆 + 青皮 + 檳榔. A classical 疝氣 formula.
- *Topical heated fennel-seed compresses* — warmed in salt or wine, applied to lower abdomen for cold pain.
- *Compound formulas* — 小茴香 figures in numerous warming / cold-dispersing compound formulas for chronic abdominal pain and dysmenorrhea of cold pattern.#footnote[Formula sources: Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009); 天臺烏藥散 from _Yīxué Fāmíng_.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Madhurikā cūrṇa* — the simple powder, ~1–3 g taken with warm water or milk; for digestive and lactation use.
- *Mukhavāsa* — the canonical post-meal practice: roasted fennel seeds alone, or with rock sugar (_miśrī_), cardamom, clove, dried mint; the after-Indian-meal _saunf_ ritual.
- *Saunf-arka / saunf-water* — distillate / decoction for eye washes (the classical _cakṣuṣya_ application) and as a gentle infant gripe-water (folk).
- *Pañca-tikta ghṛta, Mahā-tikta ghṛta* — bitter-tonic ghee preparations including madhurikā / śatapuṣpā in some recensions.
- *Triphalādya ghṛta, Drākṣādi kvātha* — Pitta-pacifying preparations including madhurikā.
- *Madhurikā-bhāvita preparations* — many digestive-Pitta-pacifying compounds use madhurikā _bhāvanā_ (trituration).#footnote[Formula sources: _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_; _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I. The mukhavāsa role is universal in modern South Asian dietetics.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

A particularly rich classical European tradition.

- *Greco-Roman:* _Theophrastus_ and the Hippocratic Corpus use μάραθον (_máraθon_, fennel) widely. _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_ III.70, _máraθon_) treats fennel as a major drug — diuretic, lactagogue, eye-strengthening (the famous Greek and European use), antifebrile, antispasmodic, anti-asthmatic; the *eye* use is striking and is the basis of the medieval European "fennel for eyesight" tradition. _Pliny_ (_Naturalis Historia_ XX) catalogs many uses, retelling the story that serpents shed their old skin and recover their eyesight by rubbing against fennel — the classical origin of the eyesight-fennel connection. _Galen_ classes fennel as warming and drying.
- *Etymology of Marathon:* the Greek place-name *Marathon* (Μαραθών) is derived from μάραθον, meaning "fennel-field" — the famous battle site (490 BCE) was a fennel-grown plain. The Marathon-fennel etymology is the most famous toponymic spice connection in classical history.
- *Roman cookery and medicine:* fennel was a canonical Roman culinary herb; Apicius and Cato use it extensively; it is also a staple of Mediterranean fish cookery, descending continuously to modern Italian and French cuisine.
- *Medieval European:* fennel was one of the *quattuor semina calida minora* ("four lesser warm seeds": anise, caraway, cumin, fennel) — the canonical European carminative quartet. Used widely in monastic medicine.
- *Hildegard von Bingen* (_Physica_, late 12th c.) praises fennel ("Fenchel") at length: "Eaten daily on an empty stomach, it brings good digestion, good breath, and clarity of the eyes" — a remarkable cross-system convergence with the Ayurvedic _mukhavāsa_ + _cakṣuṣya_ uses, arrived at independently. Hildegard's fennel passages are among the most-cited praise in medieval pharmacy.
- *Anglo-Saxon and Old English:* fennel ("finugl") is one of the *nine sacred herbs* of the Lacnunga manuscript's nine-herbs charm — venerated and used for protection.
- *Galenic / pharmacy preparations:* _Aqua Foeniculi_ (fennel water, the classical infant / weaning preparation); _Spiritus Foeniculi_; _Oleum Foeniculi_; _Syrupus Foeniculi_. The infant-colic *Gripe Water* tradition (chiefly in British and South Asian colonial pharmacy) is centrally a fennel preparation, with dill / fennel oils — the descendant of Aqua Foeniculi.
- *Modern Western herbal use:* persists in cough syrups, digestive teas (after-dinner fennel tea is a major Mediterranean tradition), and lactation teas. ESCOP and Commission E accept fennel for dyspepsia and as a galactagogue.
- *Anise-fennel distillate spirits:* fennel is a contributor (with anise and other aromatics) to many Mediterranean anise-spirits — sometimes labeled "absinthe-style" (though true absinthe is wormwood-anise-fennel: _Artemisia absinthium_ + _Pimpinella anisum_ + _Foeniculum vulgare_, the *"holy trinity"* of absinthe distillation).#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.70 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XX (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_; Theophrastus, _Historia Plantarum_; Hippocrates _passim_. Marathon etymology: standard Greek toponymy. Apicius, _De Re Coquinaria_; Cato, _De Agri Cultura_. Medieval and Salernitan _quattuor semina calida minora_: standard pharmacy histories. Hildegard: _Physica_ (Throop translation). Anglo-Saxon Nine Herbs Charm: _Lacnunga_ manuscript (10th–11th c.). Galenic preparations: pharmacopoeial history; Gripe Water: colonial pharmacy literature. The absinthe "holy trinity" (wormwood-anise-fennel) is standard distillation history.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Volatile oil (2–7% of dried fruit):* dominantly *trans-anethole* (~60–80%, especially in sweet fennel) and *fenchone* (5–30%, especially in bitter fennel), with *estragole (methyl chavicol)* (~3–6%), *limonene*, *α-pinene*, *β-myrcene*, *p-anisaldehyde*, *cis-anethole* (trace, considered toxic; bitter cultivars).
- *Fixed oil* (~15–20%).
- *Flavonoids*: quercetin, rutin, kaempferol, isoquercitrin.
- *Phenolic acids*: chlorogenic acid, ferulic acid, rosmarinic acid.
- *Coumarins*: bergapten, scopoletin, umbelliferone.
- *Sterols*: stigmasterol, β-sitosterol.
- *Minerals*: calcium, potassium, iron, manganese — notable in the seed.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); ESCOP and Commission E monographs on Foeniculi fructus; reviews in _Phytochemistry_ and _Journal of Essential Oil Research_. The sweet/bitter cultivar distinction is the principal practical chemical lever.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Carminative / antispasmodic:* trans-anethole and fenchone have documented antispasmodic activity on intestinal smooth muscle; the cross-system Dioscoridean / Bencao / Ayurvedic convergent action. Clinical evidence in functional dyspepsia and IBS is supportive; meta-analyses cluster around modest-to-moderate effect.
- *Galactagogue:* phytoestrogenic anethole / dianethole activity; folk lactation-tea use has modest modern support.
- *Eye-tonic (modern restatement):* the antioxidant flavonoid fraction is consistent with the classical _cakṣuṣya_ and Dioscoridean eye-tonic use; clinical evidence preliminary.
- *Anti-inflammatory:* preclinical; modest clinical evidence in dysmenorrhea (where fennel performs comparable to mefenamic acid in small RCTs).
- *Mild estrogenic:* relevant in lactation and hormonal-sensitivity contexts.
- *Antimicrobial:* in vitro broad-spectrum; modest clinical relevance.
- *Estragole consideration:* sweet fennel cultivars are lower in estragole (< 5% of oil) than bitter cultivars (~5–10%); the EU regulatory framework limits high-dose estragole exposure; whole-seed and infusion use raise no concern; concentrated oil use requires moderation.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical convergence on *digestive carminative + antispasmodic + lactation + eye-tonic* across Greco-Roman, Ayurvedic, and (less for eye-tonic) Chinese reading is one of the cleanest cross-system convergences in the entire materia medica. The modern pharmacology supports the digestive and lactation uses well; the eye-tonic use less rigorously but plausibly.#footnote[Pharmacology and clinical evidence: ESCOP and Commission E monographs; current reviews in _Phytotherapy Research_, _Phytomedicine_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_; dysmenorrhea RCTs in Iranian and broader literature.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary / infusion use:* up to ~5 g of seed per day in food and tea is safe — the dietary register.
- *ESCOP / Commission E:* 5–7 g seed per day in dyspepsia; 0.05–0.2 g essential oil per day; preparation as needed for cough or lactation.
- *Essential oil:* in pharmaceutical preparation as flavoring (mg quantities); *not for direct ingestion* of multi-mL quantities.
- *Gripe-water / infant colic:* traditional and ESCOP-accepted (in fennel-water form, not high-essential-oil form); pediatric use should be brief and at reasonable doses.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) known *estrogen-sensitive cancer* / *endometriosis* (theoretical caution); (b) *pregnancy* at high-essential-oil doses (culinary safe); (c) heavy chronic essential-oil use of *bitter-fennel* form (estragole concern); (d) Apiaceae allergy; (e) infants — pediatric use should be moderate and in dilute fennel-water form, not concentrated oil.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: ESCOP _Monographs_; Commission E; WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_, vol. 3 — _Fructus Foeniculi_. Sweet-vs-bitter cultivar distinction is the principal practical chemical lever for estragole.]

